Title: Judge's Crossword Puzzle No. 8
Author: Lucille A. O'Brien
Date: 1924-12-20
Byline: The above Puzzle was contributed by Lucille A. O'Brien, 327½ South Hill St., Los Angeles, Cal.
Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1924-12-20_87/page/n34/mode/1up
Puzzle-Number: 8
Clue-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1924-12-20_87/page/n33/mode/1up
Solution-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1924-12-27_87/page/n34/mode/1up


#SHEET#SPREE#
BAA#NEWPA#HAT
ED#RUN#ICE#RE
D#T#FENCE#T#N
#SEA#TIE#BAT#
SMASH#N#HABIT
OI#HOPEFUL#PI
STEEP#P#GLASS
#HAS#RIM#SPY#
T#T#RENIG#E#M
OR#GAS#CAR#BE
POP#KICKS#TAN
#TAKEN#SPOON#


A1. What kind of music is snoring? ~ SHEET
A5. A weekly event with some men (not bath). ~ SPREE
A9. A bleat. ~ BAA
A10. A stepfather (two words). ~ NEWPA
A11. Something to watch in a restaurant. ~ HAT
A13. What the boys call Edward. ~ ED
A14. What kids do when they break windows. ~ RUN
A15. "There are sermons in stones," and this kind of cream in bricks. ~ ICE
A16. Concerning (abrv.). ~ RE
A18. What runs around a yard that women gossip over? ~ FENCE
A21. What gets rough with the sailors? ~ SEA
A23. Something you pull and hit the apple. (No, William Tell, the word isn't bow.) ~ TIE
A24. Made Babe Ruth famous. ~ BAT
A26. When two road hogs meet. ~ SMASH
A28. Something that is very hard to break. ~ HABIT
A30. The way an Irishman says "I." ~ OI
A31. Chief characteristic of an old maid. ~ HOPEFUL
A32. A Greek letter (often associated with Eata Bitea). ~ PI
A33. The kind of a hill that Fords regret. ~ STEEP
A35. People who live in these kind of houses should pull down the shades. ~ GLASS
A37. Third person, singular of a verb meaning possession. ~ HAS
A38. Supposed to be demountable. ~ RIM
A40. A game played by reformers. ~ SPY
A42. What you do when you don't "ante." ~ RENIG
A45. A conjunction. ~ OR
A47. What does your car run out of that your wife doesn't? ~ GAS
A48. Something that has made the old parlor sofa a thing of the past. ~ CAR
A49. To exist. ~ BE
A50. The kind of bottles an umpire makes a good target for. ~ POP
A52. What a chorus girl does to get a raise. ~ KICKS
A53. An inexpensive kind of coat (worn in summer). ~ TAN
A54. What you get in a poker game. ~ TAKEN
A55. What the "Book of Etiquette" says should be used instead of a fork when you eat soup. ~ SPOON

D1. How does paying your income tax make you feel (besides broke). ~ SAD
D2. A villainous exclamation. ~ HA
D3. Synonym for sufficient (phonetic). ~ ENUF
D4. A doctrine or dogma maintained as being true. ~ TENET
D5. Desired in soup and musical comedies. ~ SPICE
D6. Something to keep up (not trousers). ~ PACE
D7. Exclamation of the hard-of-hearing. ~ EH
D8. Good advice goes in one and out of the other. ~ EAR
D9. Something to make in the morning. ~ BED
D12. Something that comes between nine and eleven (not bedtime story). ~ TEN
D17. The kind of fight ladies enjoy. ~ TEA
D19. What you hit if you don't roll off the alley. ~ NINEPIN
D20. What wives keep on their husbands. ~ TAB
D21. A maker of cough drops. ~ SMITH
D22. What you carry out of your cellar in the spring. ~ ASHES
D24. Three in front of every pawn shop. ~ BALLS
D25. What you get when you drink too much. (Don't be confused with what you get from the wife under the same circumstances). ~ TIPSY
D26. A distress signal. ~ SOS
D27. What the world fliers took from Los Angeles to San Francisco. ~ HOP
D28. Something that goes with a kiss. ~ HUG
D29. "It is" (contraction). ~ TIS
D34. Something you really shouldn't do with a knife. ~ EAT
D36. A quadrumane. ~ APE
D38. Used on the canvas of a fight ring. ~ RESIN
D39. 99 44/100 per cent. of all policemen. ~ MICKS
D41. Farthest away from the bottom. ~ TOP
D42. What you do to autumn leaves. ~ RAKE
D43. Something a cold shower makes you do. ~ GASP
D44. A product of the great open spaces. ~ MEN
D46. Decay. ~ ROT
D49. What blue laws do. ~ BAN
D51. The one who pays the bills. ~ PA
D53. A preposition. ~ TO


Printed with puzzle:
Judge will run a Crossword Puzzle every week and will pay $25 for each one printed, but they must be funny. None will be returned unless stamps are enclosed.

